t 18 months \" on their premium brands , <ei32 pricing> their product as much as 25 cents a 12-pack below Anheuser 's Budweiser label in many markets . Anheuser <ei33 said> it 's discounting policy basically would <ei34 involve> <ei35 matching> such <ei36 moves> by rivals on a market-by-market basis . Anheuser - Busch <ei37 announced> its <ei38 plan> at the same time it <ei39 reported> third-quarter net income <ei41 rose> a lower-than-anticipated 5.2 % to $ 238.3 million , or 83 cents a share , from $ 226.5 million , or 78 cents . Third-period sales were $ 2.49 billion , up from last year 's $ 2.34 billion . Anheuser <ei42 said> its new strategy -- <ei43 started> in some markets last month and <ei45 expected> to be <ei46 applied> soon in selected markets nationwide -- will <ei47 mean> lower-than-anticipated earnings for the last half of 1989 and for 1990 . The <ei50 projection> <ei51 sent> Anheuser shares <ei52 plunging> $ 4.375 in New York Stock Exchange composite trading yesterday . The stock <ei54 closed> at $ 38.50 on heavy volume of about 3.5 million shares . Shares of Coors , the company 's sole publicly traded major competitor , <ei55 fell> $ 1.50 apiece to $ 19.125 in national over-the-counter trading , apparently on investor concerns over potential <ei56 fallout> from the coming pricing <ei57 struggle> . Anheuser <ei58 noted> that \" beer industry sales volume is 1989 is <ei60 following> the trend that has <ei61 characterized> the last half of the ' 80s , with sales volume being essentially <ei63 flat> \" while <ei64 consolidation> <ei65 creates> fewer , bigger players . \" We can not <ei66 permit> a further <ei67 slowing> in our volume trend , \" Anheuser <ei68 said> , <ei69 adding> it will <ei70 take> \" appropriate competitive pricing <ei71 actions> to <ei72 support> our long-term market share growth strategy \" for the premium brands . Anheuser <ei73 said> it <ei74 continues> to <ei75 hold> to its <ei76 earlier-announced> goal of a 50 % U.S. market share by the mid-1990s . Beneath the tepid news-release jargon <ei78 lies> a powerful threat from the brewing giant , which last year <ei79 accounted> for about 41 % of all U.S. beer sales and is <ei80 expected> to <ei81 see> that <ei82 grow> to 42.5 % in the current year . \" Anheuser is the biggest guy in the bar , and he just <ei84 decided> to <ei85 join> in the barroom <ei86 brawl> , \" <ei87 said> Joseph J. Doyle , an analyst with Smith Barney , Harris Upham amp Co. \" It 's going to <ei88 get> bloody . \" Jerry Steinman , publisher of Beer Marketers Insights , a trade newsletter , <ei89 said> Anheuser 's <ei90 announcement> <ei91 means> \" everybody else in the industry is going to <ei92 have> a difficult time <ei93 reaching> their profit objectives . \" Prudential - Bache Securities Inc. analyst George E. Thompson <ei94 downplayed> the importance of the <ei95 announcement> , and <ei96 called> any comparison between the coming beer-industry tiff and the seemingly unending \" cola wars , \" unwarranted . Mr. Thompson <ei97 calls> discounting \" a loser 's game for anyone without a dominant market share , \" and <ei98 projected> that Anheuser 's statement of intent could simply be a means of <ei99 warning> competitors to <ei100 ease> up on <ei101 price-cutting> or face a costly and fruitless <ei102 battle> . Mr. Thompson <ei103 noted> that the disappointing earnings , which <ei104 fell> five cents a share short of his own <ei105 projections> , <ei106 contributed> to the sell-off by an edgy and currently unforgiving investing public . But Smith Barney 's Mr. Doyle , who yesterday <ei108 trimmed> his 1990 Anheuser earnings projection to $ 2.95 a share from $ 3.10 , <ei110 called> the market 's reaction \" justified . \" While the third-quarter earnings were a \" moderate disappointment , \" he <ei112 said> , \" the real bad news is the <ei113 intensity> of price <ei114 competition> \" in the premium-beer sector . <ei115 According> to Mr. Steinman , the newsletter publ